<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Histiaeus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 493 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Histiaeus was the tyrant of Miletus under the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> in the late 6th century BCE. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> he guarded Darius's bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dariuss-scythian-campaign">Scythian campaign</a> and was later kept at the Persian court, from where he encouraged his deputy and son-in-law Aristagoras to start the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> in 499 BCE. He was captured and executed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in 493 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/histiaeus.jpg" alt="Histiaeus" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IONIA,_Miletos._Circa_600-550_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
